\title{Pseudocode}
\date{2026-09-04T13:29:10Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{In class, we will often use \em{pseudocode}, which is like real
code except that it can include English phrases to avoid specifying
too much detail.  The rule of thumb is that a competent Data
Structures student should be able to translate the English phrases
into real, working code without difficulty or ambiguity.  For example,
this pseudocode is perfectly fine:}

\p{\acode{\startverb
\> foo($l$) = \\
\> \tb \> !for each item $x$ in the list $l$: \\
\>     \> \tb \> Add $1$ to $d[x]$
\stopverb
}}

\p{since we assume everyone knows how to do things like loop over every element in a list, increment a value in a dictionary, and so on.  On the other hand, here is an example of pseudocode that leaves out too much detail:}

\p{\acode{\startverb
\> bar($t$) = \\
\> \tb \> !for each node $x$ in the binary tree $t$: \\
\>     \> \tb \> Find the node $y$ such that the lowest common ancestor of $x$ and $y$ is as close as possible to $3$ \\
\>     \>     \> Increment the value stored at $y$
\stopverb
}}

\p{It is not obvious how to iterate over every node in a binary tree, and it is even less obvious how to carry out the selection of #{y} in the second line.}
